      "mother": {
        "preparation": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "preparation",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Develop a standalone, low-overhead streaming-DP candidate. Replace the two DP rows and copying with one array: initialize dp[0]=1, then scan each row with left=0; an open cell becomes dp[j]+left modulo MOD, while a blocked cell sets both dp[j] and left to zero. Since operands are reduced, replace remainder with one conditional subtraction; their sum fits signed 32 bits. Investigate faster input and sorted, deduplicated, row-indexed obstacles, processing obstacle-free intervals without a per-cell obstacle lookup. Compare preprocessing costs against a compact bitmap alternative without assuming either wins. Avoid unconditional quadratic obstacle storage for large valid grids. Preserve endpoint blocking, duplicate-coordinate semantics and N=1 behavior. Report end-to-end measurements under the unchanged harness.",
            "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm with a general fallback. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1000000007 through 2N-2; this range is below the modulus. Sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates. For each blocked point and finally the destination, compute unrestricted binomial path counts minus contributions from earlier coordinate-wise reachable blocked points: ways[i] = C(r_i+c_i,r_i) - sum_j ways[j]*C(r_i-r_j+c_i-c_j,r_i-r_j). Reduce all arithmetic safely using 64-bit products. Handle blocked endpoints explicitly. With no blockers, use the unrestricted binomial count. After sorting, this costs O(N+K^2) for K distinct blockers; the density constraint does not make K universally small. Supply a correct streaming-DP fallback and research a conservative crossover based on general operation and memory estimates, not particular test identities. Measure the complete candidate, including parsing, sorting and factorial preparation.",
            "C": "Research four-core CPU parallelism through tiled anti-diagonal wavefront DP, using only facilities supported by the fixed build. Tiles depend on their top and left neighbors; tiles on one anti-diagonal are independent. Explore an O(N)-space boundary implementation: one array holds the latest bottom boundary for each tile-column segment, and another holds the latest right boundary for each tile-row segment. Each tile updates its disjoint segments in place, with synchronization between diagonals. Use a persistent worker team rather than launching threads per tile, and compact row-indexed obstacle data. Investigate tile sizes, locality, load balance and synchronization overhead on ARM64; do not assume four threads beat the serial kernel at this baseline duration. Retain a serial path for insufficient work or unsupported threading. Deliver a standalone correct candidate and measured crossover evidence.",
            "G": "Integrate only demonstrated improvements from the research trees. First compare each standalone candidate with the baseline; then measure combinations separately, since shared preprocessing, dispatch, cache behavior and threading can erase gains. Investigate a shared input/obstacle representation and a general dispatcher between sparse combinatorial counting, serial streaming DP and tiled parallel DP. Base selection on mathematical work estimates and measured overhead, never recognized test values or stored answers. Retain the fastest validated standalone candidate if integration loses. Use the unchanged correctness and timing harness, recording end-to-end results rather than kernel-only timings. Review N=1, blocked endpoints, duplicate blockers, obstacle-free grids, unreachable destinations, modular arithmetic and memory bounds. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions or budgets.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem D, grid-path counting; A/B/C here are research roles, not the other contest problems. The baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 0.08038 seconds across three closely grouped measurements. No workload dimensions or profiling are supplied, so bottlenecks remain hypotheses. The source performs O(N^2) cell updates, stores an O(N^2)-bit obstacle grid, copies a DP row after every iteration, uses remainder per cell and leaves synchronized stream input enabled. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. The complementary objectives are lower-overhead exact DP, a sparse-obstacle algorithmic alternative, and dependency-correct multicore execution. Every branch must remain correct for all valid inputs; sparse density alone does not justify quadratic work in the number of blockers."
          },
          "request_sha256": "e3cc770709dc3ab7b95c799193e2711122e91246491594558f6f43f21b384633",
